   hgm_matched  steering 0.879  held-out 0.834  optimism(steer-test)=+0.045
   oracle       steering 0.950  held-out 0.888  optimism(steer-test)=+0.062
   random       steering 0.619  held-out 0.555  optimism(steer-test)=+0.064
   solves: a cheap selection signal.  limit: it is optimistic.  In OUR runs the gap is ~+0.05-0.06 for EVERY method, even random selection,
   so it is NOT growing with the number of candidates compared; it mostly reflects the dev-vs-test category shift (10% unseen-style pinch lines in test vs 3% in dev).

==============================================================================
10.17 metric 3: Recursive Learnability (our operationalisation)
==============================================================================
   planted  naive    learnability 0.226 ± 0.020  (from 6.6 categories/run)
   planted  so_rsi   learnability 0.424 ± 0.021  (from 6.6 categories/run)
   clean    naive    learnability 0.362 ± 0.029  (from 4.9 categories/run)
   clean    so_rsi   learnability 0.457 ± 0.024  (from 6.6 categories/run)
   solves: flags proposers stuck on tasks that always fail or are already solved.  limit: depends on the task taxonomy; says nothing about whether outcomes are GOOD.

==============================================================================
10.17 metric 4: swarm diversity (Jaccard)
==============================================================================
   budget 3000: swarm 0.25   mutation-only 0.46
   budget 6000: swarm 0.09   mutation-only 0.18
   solves: detects premature convergence.  limit: diversity of genomes != diversity of behaviour; here lower diversity went with equal accuracy, not worse.

WHY SO MANY METRICS: each system changes a different object (scaffold, proposer, workflow, swarm); no single number covers all of them.


######## lab_10_01_claim_vs_evidence.py ########
# 10.01 Claim vs evidence - Budget RSI (arXiv:2610.29121, Darzi, Balija, He; submitted 21 Oct 2026)

**Sources actually read:** the arXiv abstract page and fragments of the HTML version. I did *not* read the full tables, so
figures marked UNVERIFIED are carried over from the course repo's summary and may be wrong.

## Headline vs. what was measured vs. what is untested
| # | Claim as stated | What supports it (paper side) | Conditions that limit it | What our laptop adaptation preserves / changes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | A linear readout on the parent's steering-split solve rate plus eight diff coordinates, fitted from 96 scaffold edits labelled on a 16-instance fit split and then **frozen**, can rank proposed edits before they are evaluated. | Method description in the abstract/HTML: one readout per run, coefficients fixed through 24 keep steps, archive of four nodes; steering split = 48 (HumanEval+) / 64 (Polyglot) instances. | Coding agent = GPT-4o on two benchmarks; the 8 coordinates are the paper's own diff features. | Preserved: frozen linear readout, 96 calibration edits, archive of 4, 24 keep steps, 8 proposals/step. Changed: edits are patches to a parser (not an LLM rewriting a scaffold); diff coordinates are our 8 real unified-diff counts; fit/steering = 24/80. |
| 2 | Under **matched checker-evaluation budgets** (readout & ablation: 96 calibration edits + 24 children; HGM selection: 144 children on Polyglot, 176 on HumanEval+) the readout is competitive or best. | Budget-matching statement in the HTML; "on HumanEval+ with GPT-4o the readout is the highest mean". Course summary says 87.0% vs 84.2% - **UNVERIFIED**. | One model, two benchmarks; I could not check variance or significance. | We matched budgets in instance-evaluations. Result here: readout minus HGM-matched = **-0.238 [-0.290, -0.189]** (held-out, 30 seeds): the claim did **not** carry over to our testbed. |
| 3 | Scoring *before* running saves evaluation cost without losing selection quality. | Cost accounting in the abstract; the readout never evaluates rejected proposals. | Calibration cost is paid per run. | In our accounting calibration was **62%** of the readout method's cost. Mechanism check: the top-scored edit beat its parent 43% of the time vs 23% for a random pick - the scorer *does* rank, but not enough to pay for itself. |

## What the paper does not (as far as I can see) test, and what we did about it
* **Does a frozen readout transfer across runs?** We calibrated once on another world and reused it: readout-amortized vs HGM at the same search-only budget -> see lab 10.05 (no advantage).
* **Are the 8 coordinates sufficient?** Our readout cannot tell `digits_stripper` (catastrophic) from `collapse_spaces` (harmless): identical diff coordinates, identical score (lab 10.03 walk-through).
* **Do richer features help?** Adding an execution canary improved within-parent ranking (0.44 -> 0.49 Spearman) but did not improve end-to-end held-out accuracy; a duplicate-edit flag did not reduce wasted expansions (0.41 -> 0.44).

## Why a laptop adaptation cannot prove the headline
Different proposer, different data, different scale, one seed family. It can show a mechanism *works at ranking level* (it does) and show
where it stops paying (it does not at our cost accounting). It cannot confirm or refute the GPT-4o numbers.

## Quiz answers (own words)
**Q1.** Can the paper claim linear-readout scoring works for all code-editing tasks? No - it evaluated one model on two benchmarks.
**Q2.** Why synthetic edits in the course demo? To run without the benchmark and model. Caveat found while auditing: the course's demo generates
`held_out = 6 x steering + noise`, so held-out is correlated with the proxy *by construction*, and its "88% cost reduction" is hand-written text, not measured.



