# 10.02 Paper audits (template from the course)

## A. Budget RSI - arXiv:2610.29121
| Aspect | Paper's setup | Laptop adaptation | What is preserved |
|---|---|---|---|
| Changed object | a coding agent's scaffold (code edits selected before evaluation) | the source text of an ingredient parser, patched by real edits | edits are code diffs; selection happens before execution |
| Feedback type | parent's steering-split solve rate + 8 diff coordinates -> linear readout; labels from a 16-instance fit split | same inputs; fit split = 24, steering = 80 | one frozen linear readout per run |
| Evaluation protocol | steering split 48 (HumanEval+) / 64 (Polyglot); 24 keep steps; archive of 4; HGM Thompson selection baseline | held-out 400-instance test never used for selection; 30 fresh seeds, paired CIs | steering/held-out separation |
| Compute budget | equal checker evaluations across methods | equal instance evaluations (Meter) | matched-budget comparison |
| Untested assumption | the frozen readout stays useful (and pays for its calibration) | tested: readout minus HGM-matched = -0.238; readout vs parent-rate ablation -0.006 | - |
What a real test would need: the actual benchmark, the paper's proposer, and its diff features.

## B. SO-RSI - arXiv:2610.05701 (Jiang, Vempaty, Jagmohan; submitted 5 Oct 2026)
| Aspect | Paper's setup | Laptop adaptation | What is preserved |
|---|---|---|---|
| Changed object | the RSI *workflow* (outer RSI) for Lean 4 proving and Verus verified-code generation | parser source; the loop's edit choices | the improvement loop, not just the artifact |
| Feedback type | execution traces, executable diagnostic probes, persistent inquiry memory | accept/reject traces, a causal stage-ablation probe, an InquiryMemory | anomaly -> probe -> memory -> structural edit |
| Evaluation protocol | held-out pass rates under matched 24-hour search budgets; reported +21.8 (Lean) / +25.8 (Verus) points over Naive RSI | held-out accuracy at matched evaluation budget (probe cost charged), 30 seeds | matched budget, held-out |
| Compute budget | 24 h per comparison | 8000 instance evaluations | - |
| Untested assumption | the paper notes Naive RSI *already* does some diagnostic inquiry; SO-RSI makes it systematic | tested with a third arm: first-order + BLIND structural moves. At budget 8000 (planted start): naive 0.485, naive+blind 0.794, SO-RSI 0.977 - diagnosis adds beyond merely having structural moves | - |
Caveats we found: our base scaffold contains a *planted* blocker; plain naive RSI cannot delete code it did not write, which inflates the headline gap
(+0.49); the fair gap is vs naive+blind (+0.18). Probes cost ~31% of the budget and 90% returned no blocker.

## C. Self-HPO - arXiv:2610.22791 (Wei, Yao, Peng et al., Microsoft Research)
| Aspect | Paper's setup | Laptop adaptation | What is preserved |
|---|---|---|---|
| Changed object | harness AND policy of a coding agent | harness = (guard, search set); policy = softmax over edits | two coupled objects, alternating |
| Feedback type | the policy analyses its own failures (H-step); RL under the chosen harness (P-step); hard tasks become the next search set | failure mining on a pool; REINFORCE; hard-set mode | self-proposed harness, evolving search set |
| Evaluation protocol | SWE-bench Verified / Multilingual / Pro; reported average 57.8% -> 64.8% (Qwen3.5-35B-A3B) | held-out accuracy, 4 conditions, budget sweep, 30 seeds | policy-only and harness-only ablations |
| Compute budget | RL training runs | instance evaluations; H-step probes charged | - |
| Untested assumption | joint gain exceeds the sum of parts (the paper's cross-evaluation reports compounding) | tested: synergy at 12000 = -0.016 [-0.060, +0.025] -> not super-additive here | - |



######## lab_10_09_compare_scaling.py ########
# 10.09 Comparing AReaL-RSI-style and SwarmRSI-style scaling (measured on the ingredient-parser testbed)

Mechanisms are from the course's summaries of arXiv:2610.18083 and arXiv:2610.20431 (not the papers); our
simulations are small, so every number below is a statement about *our miniature*, not about the papers.

| Aspect | AReaL-RSI style (leader + researchers) | SwarmRSI style (peer swarm) |
|---|---|---|
| Coordination | leader chooses an orientation; researchers test edits | 5 peer genomes mutate and recombine |
| Memory | conditional: supported / refuted / unresolved findings, shared | the population itself; recombination imports peers' patches |
| Selection | leader integrates supported findings | mu+lambda survival on steering accuracy |
| **Measured advantage** | shared memory beat independent researchers by +0.043 [+0.004, +0.083] at budget 1500 and +0.112 [+0.073, +0.152] at 2500 (it removes wasted repeats: 2.3 repeats/run without sharing, 0 with) | beat random search by a wide margin (0.930 vs 0.753 at budget 6000) |
| **Measured limitation** | the advantage vanishes once budget is generous (-0.018 at 4000, CI [-0.038, +0.004]) | recombination added nothing over mutation alone (0.930 vs 0.948) and collapsed diversity (0.09 vs 0.18 Jaccard) |
| Choose when | budget is tight and several workers could duplicate each other | the search space is broad and you want many independent starting points; check that recombination is actually pulling its weight |

**One-sentence takeaway.** Coordination (shared memory) helps most when work is scarce and duplication is the main waste; population
mechanisms need a measurement of whether their *recombination* step earns its keep, because here it did not.



######## lab_10_12_compare_workflow.py ########
# 10.12 RSIFlow-style vs Self-HPO-style adaptation (measured on the ingredient-parser testbed)

RSIFlow mechanism: course summary of arXiv:2610.21791 (not the paper). Self-HPO: arXiv:2610.22791 (abstract verified).
Our implementations are miniatures: numbers describe *this testbed only*.

| Aspect | RSIFlow style | Self-HPO style |
|---|---|---|
| What adapts | the workflow's stage routing (dedup / guard / diagnose) | the harness (guard + search set) AND the policy's edit preferences |
| Trigger | measured waste in the last 8 cycles | every round (H-step then P-step) |
| What learns | nothing numerical: rule-based meta-agent | the policy by REINFORCE; the harness proposer by a bandit |
| **Measured benefit** | beat the static pipeline by +0.250 [+0.172, +0.337] at budget 6000 | at budget 12000: joint vs no-adaptation +0.034 [-0.032, +0.094] (not distinguishable from 0) |
| **Measured cost / limit** | lost to simply turning every stage on from the start (-0.245 [-0.328, -0.164]) because of detection latency (stages switched on at cycles 17/29/48) | the H-step is a fixed overhead: at budget 6000 it took 85% of the budget and the joint method scored 0.352 vs 0.691 for no adaptation |
| Paper claim 'joint > sum of parts' | n/a | NOT reproduced here: synergy at 12000 = -0.016 [-0.060, +0.025] |
| Prefer when | stages are costly to run all the time and the bottleneck really changes | there is a learnable policy AND a harness worth searching, and budget is large enough to amortise the H-step |

**Takeaway.** Both ideas hinge on an overhead-versus-benefit trade that the headline numbers hide. The honest comparison for adaptive routing is
'always on', and for harness search it is 'spend that budget on more policy updates'.


